content.text:
Mostly yes, with one catch that is bigger than it looks.

**What a fake Bambu printer would cover.** JusPrin's shell already reports live state for Bambu printers, so the chip would show Idle, Printing, and Offline with no upstream switch at all. It would also exercise the Bambu print flow that your real users take: the Select Machine dialog, the file upload, the print command, and the AMS spool data that the Moonraker path never sees. It would not touch the Klipper path, so "all scenarios" means all Bambu scenarios. The two simulators cover different halves of the app.

**The catch.** The app does not speak to Bambu printers itself. That work is done by Bambu's closed network plugin, a separate library the app downloads on first use. It is installed in your OrcaSlicer data directory but not in JusPrin's yet. A fake Bambu printer has to satisfy that black box, which means:

- an MQTT broker over TLS on port 8883, accepting the printer login and access code, exchanging Bambu's status and command messages on the right topics;
- an FTPS file server on port 990, because the sliced file is uploaded there before the print command;
- either network discovery or manual entry of the printer's IP and access code, and the shell hides the dialog for manual entry;
- the exact message shapes the plugin expects, including a full status report of a couple of hundred fields, and on recent firmware an extra authorization handshake.

When any of that is slightly off, the plugin fails silently, and there is no source to read. My estimate is two to three times the code of the Moonraker fake, with certificates and two TLS servers on top, and a fair amount of trial and error against the plugin.

**So which is right depends on the goal.** For a cheap, transparent harness to test "send G-code and read status", the Moonraker fake already does it, at the price of that one upstream switch for the state word. For testing the Bambu experience your users actually get, a fake Bambu printer is the only way, and it would be a second simulator next to this one rather than a replacement. If you want it, I would start by installing the plugin into JusPrin's data directory and capturing what it sends to a real Bambu printer, since that traffic, not documentation, is the spec.
